    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\angle C=120^{\\circ}$.\n\nExpand the given identity and compare it with the cosine rule.",
      "worked": "Expanding the given identity gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(a+b)^2-c^2&=ab\\\\a^2+2ab+b^2-c^2&=ab\\\\c^2&=a^2+b^2+ab.\\end{align*}$$\nBy the cosine rule, $c^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\\cos C$. Comparing the expressions for $c^2$, and using $ab>0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}-2ab\\cos C&=ab\\\\\\cos C&=-\\frac12\\\\C&=120^{\\circ}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 64
